+/**
+ * In-process FIDO2 ("Security Key") support for Gecko's Android WebAuthn backend, over USB
+ * (CTAPHID) and NFC (ISO-DEP), with no GMS/microG privileged FIDO service.
+ *
+ * <p>Replaces the {@code play-services-fido} PendingIntent flow in {@code WebAuthnTokenManager}:
+ * the challenge is answered directly by a roaming security key.
+ *
+ * <p>Design notes (browser case — differs from the Proton app flow this was ported from):
+ *
+ * <ul>
+ *   <li>Origin/clientDataJSON: the CTAP2 request is signed over {@code clientDataHash}, which
+ *       Gecko itself computes as SHA-256 of the spec-serialized clientDataJSON (with the RP
+ *       origin from the WebAuthn request) and passes into the Java seam. We reuse that hash and
+ *       leave the response's clientDataJson null so Gecko substitutes its own assembled copy —
+ *       no facet/APK-key-hash origin like the app case, and no second serializer to keep in
+ *       sync.
+ *   <li>Both makeCredential (enrollment) and getAssertion (sign-in) are implemented; the
+ *       transport command byte is generalized in the transports for exactly this.
+ *   <li>up is always required; uv is sent only when the request requires it, and rk only when a
+ *       resident credential is requested (an unrequested option risks
+ *       CTAP2_ERR_UNSUPPORTED_OPTION on plain keys).
+ *   <li>Transport selection is automatic and concurrent: a plugged-in USB key and an NFC tap are
+ *       both watched, first-wins; a key attached late is picked up by the attach receiver plus a
+ *       bounded deviceList poll (OS enumeration delay).
+ * </ul>
+ *
+ * <p>Blocking CTAP2 exchanges run on a daemon worker thread; all Android UI-touching steps
+ * (reader mode, permission dialog, receivers) run on the main thread.
+ *
+ * <p>Ported (Java) from the Proton Mail fork's fido-nfc module (GPL-3.0-or-later), with the
